+## Key Design Decisions
+
+- **Use 2Q eviction for preview sheets.** The worker maintains separate queues for first-use and reused sheets so one-off sheets from long sequential screenings do not displace repeatedly revisited comparison sheets. LRU was rejected because it lets sequential scans evict useful comparison sheets. Frequency-based eviction with aging was rejected because counter maintenance and aging can retain stale popularity after editors move to another project. The extra queue bookkeeping and a tunable queue-size split remain private to the preview worker; the preview HTTP interface, render queue, and 12 GiB cache limit are unchanged.
